==============================================================================
RING NEGATIVE CONTROLS  (a broken ring, and what the hash can and cannot see)
==============================================================================
  PASS  differs, maxabs=1.221e+03 worst=p
        ring_margin='x_only', 3x3, N=1
        forgetting the y bands changes the answer, so the gate can see a broken ring at all
  PASS  differs, maxabs=9.320e+02 worst=p
        ring_margin='x_only', 1x2 y seam, N=2, nb=1
        and it still sees it on the smallest plan that has a y seam -- but only at nbuffers=1; at nbuffers=2 the prefetch hides the hazard
  PASS  bit-exact
        ring_margin='halo', 3x3, N=1
        an UNSOUND ring that is nonetheless bit-exact: the 192 cells it drops are outside the influence cone. Only rings.assert_ring_covers_reads catches this, which is why that check exists
  PASS  bit-exact
        ring_margin='halo', ragged x, N=2
        same, with 9,344 dropped cells -- the size of the violation says nothing about whether the hash notices
  OBS   bit-exact -- the card behaved as its single copy engine suggests it would
        ring_ordering='submission' (cross-stream events REMOVED), 3x3, N=3, nb=2
        MEASURED BOTH WAYS, same code, same machine, hours apart: BIT-EXACT on an idle card, and WRONG (maxabs 3.7e+02) while another process shared the GPU. The 'one DMA queue drained in submission order' argument is therefore NOT sufficient -- it is a property of an uncontended card, and a tiled run that relied on it would corrupt intermittently, depending on what else the machine was doing. This is why run_tiled defaults to ring_ordering='events'. Either outcome here is a pass; a MISMATCH is the more informative one.

